  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 AM EDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nAn active pattern remains in place through the weekend and into\nearly next week, although precipitation chances will come in \nseveral waves rather than a continuous period of rain. A weak \nmid- level disturbance may be enough to squeeze out a few \nshowers or thunderstorms early this morning, but coverage should\nremain fairly limited with any showers fighting a pretty dry \nprofile. Additional destabilization this afternoon will support \nanother chance for scattered thunderstorms, mainly across \nnortheast Lower. Large-scale forcing remains weak, so continue \nthinking that any development will likely be tied to localized \nconvergence and lake breeze boundaries. With any storms that do \ndevelop this afternoon, they could squeak out some small hail \nand some gusty winds as they pulse.\n\nAttention then turns to a cold front expected to move through \ntonight into Saturday morning, bringing the best opportunity for\na more organized band of showers and thunderstorms. Rain \nchances should gradually diminish Saturday afternoon before \nanother round of showers and thunderstorms arrives late Saturday\nnight into Sunday as deeper moisture and upper-level support \nincrease once again. \n\nThe pattern turns wetter early next week as a frontal boundary \nstalls south of the Great Lakes while several disturbances move \nthrough the broader flow. This should bring better chances for \nmore widespread rainfall Monday into at least part of Tuesday, \nwith some potential for locally heavier rainfall, especailly \nacross northern Lower. WPC currently advertising a Marginal Risk\nfor Excessive Rainfall as a result. While a lot of the finer \ndetails need to be worked out, the overall signal for a wetter \nstart to the week has remained fairly consistent. Broad \ntroughing over the region will keep temperatures near or \nslightly below normal.",
